====Preposition====
{{en-head|prep}}

# {{n-g|Introducing a basis of comparison, with an object in the objective case.}}
#: {{ux|en|You are not as tall '''as''' my sister.}}
#: {{ux|en|They are big '''as''' houses.}}
#* {{RQ:Belloc Lowndes Lodger|I|0016
|passage=A great bargain also had been the excellent Axminster carpet which covered the floor; '''as''', again, the arm-chair in which Bunting now sat forward, staring into the dull, small fire.}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=1|passage=She was like a Beardsley ''Salome'', he had said. And indeed she had the narrow eyes and the high cheekbone of that creature, and '''as''' nearly the sinuosity as is compatible with human symmetry.}}
# In the [[role]] of.
#: {{ux|en|What is your opinion '''as''' a parent?}}
#: {{ux|en|'''As''' your big sis, I worry about your life too.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was never seen '''as''' the boss, but rather '''as''' a friend.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Tom Pendergast; Sara Pendergast|title=St. James encyclopedia of popular culture|volume=2|page=223
|passage=Directed by Howard Hawks, ''Gentlemen Prefer Blondes'' starred Marilyn Monroe '''as''' Lorelei and Jane Russell '''as''' Dorothy.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/catherine-clabby Catherine Clabby]
|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/focus-on-everything Focus on Everything]
|passage=Not long ago, it was difficult to produce photographs of tiny creatures with every part in focus.{{...}}A photo processing technique called focus stacking has changed that. Developed '''as''' a tool to electronically combine the sharpest bits of multiple digital images, focus stacking is a boon to biologists seeking full focus on a micron scale.}}
# In the [[form]] of.
#: {{ux|en|I bought you a new toy '''as''' a special treat.}}

=====Usage notes=====
In traditional standard English {{m|en||as}} (like {{m|en|than}}) is a conjunction, not a preposition. The use of pronominal case forms (subject vs. object) therefore depends on the syntactical context. Compare:
* {{ux|en|She loves you just as much as '''I''' [do].}}
* {{ux|en|She loves you just as much as [she loves] '''me'''.}}
In modern everyday English, this difference may be lost and the use of bare subject forms (''I, he, she, we, they'') after {{m|en||as}} may seem pedantic. Only the object forms are used on their own.
* {{ux|en|You are not as tall as '''I'''. ''(formal/dated short form)''}}
* {{ux|en|You are not as tall as '''me'''. ''(informal short form)''}}
* {{ux|en|You are not as tall as '''I am'''. ''(full form)''}}

